`<Admin>` is the whole editor: a rail of collections, a panel with the open collection's documents (or the open document's outline), a top bar with Save, the preview, and an inspector with the selected item's form. It's a component, so you mount it on a route of your own.

```tsx
import { Admin } from '@mortar-kit/admin';

export default function AdminRoute() {
  return (
    <Admin
      bricks={bricks}
      collections={collections}
      content={adminContent}
      previewUrl="/admin/preview"
    />
  );
}
```

`content` is a `Content`: reads plus `save` and `remove`. Give it uncached reads, so the admin always shows what is saved. Saves are validated again by `createContent`, and problems come back to the editor, linked to the fields they're about.

## The preview route

The preview is an iframe on your own origin, rendering `<PreviewPage>` with your bricks and styles. Pass `layouts` to render each collection's documents inside your real layouts, so a post previews with its title and date:

```tsx
import { PreviewPage } from '@mortar-kit/admin/preview';

const layouts = {
  posts: (doc, body) => <PostLayout post={doc}>{body}</PostLayout>,
  products: (doc) => <ProductLayout product={doc} />,
};

export default function AdminPreview() {
  return <PreviewPage bricks={bricks} content={freshSource} layouts={layouts} />;
}
```

Load both routes with `lazy()`, so the admin's code is only downloaded on these routes.

## The location in your URL

By default the admin keeps track of where it is itself. To put it in your URL, so documents can be linked and back and forward move between them, pass `location` and `onNavigate`:

```tsx
const [params, setParams] = useSearchParams();

<Admin
  bricks={bricks}
  collections={collections}
  content={adminContent}
  previewUrl="/admin/preview"
  location={{ collection: params.collection ?? 'pages', id: params.id }}
  onNavigate={(to) => setParams({ collection: to.collection, id: to.id ?? null })}
/>
```

## Unsaved changes

The editor guards closing the tab. To guard navigation inside your app, listen to `onDirtyChange` and block with the router's `useBeforeLeave` while there are unsaved changes.

## Editing one document

`<Editor>` is the editor for a single document, without the rail and the list. Give it the collection, the document, `previewUrl` and an `onSave`.
